    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7",
      "function_role": "harmonic-anticipation-target",
      "narrative": "For any chord quality approached by consecutive eighth-note groupings, Bergonzi's harmony anticipation rule prescribes that late displacement positions (#6, #7, #8 for a four-note group; #5, #6, #7 for five-note groups) automatically anticipate the next chord \u2014 either by approaching its notes, stating it directly, or letting the final eighth note sound the chord. 'For #6, #7, and #8, the harmony can be anticipated. That is, starting on the \"and\" of three, beat four or the \"and\" of four, you can anticipate the next chord by either approaching notes of the next chord or by simply sounding the next chord.' Chapter 3, p. 26.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "jerry-bergonzi:melodic-rhythms-vol-4:consecutive-note-displacement"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "melodic-rhythms-vol-4",
