      "granularity_name": "rhythmic-placement"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7",
      "function_role": "accent-framework-fill-approach",
      "narrative": "Bergonzi prescribes first playing only accents through a tune (as if part of a horn section), then slowly filling in around those accents across several choruses. 'Try taking any tune and playing just accents as if you were the saxophone or trumpet section in a big band. After playing through several choruses, slowly fill in around the accents.' Chapter 27, p. 110.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "jerry-bergonzi:melodic-rhythms-vol-4:rhythmic-grammar"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "melodic-rhythms-vol-4",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "melodic-rhythms-vol-4",
          "citation": "chapter 27 p. 110"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "i",
      "granularity_name": "other"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7",
      "function_role": "note-grouping-density-awareness",
      "narrative": "Bergonzi prescribes developing awareness of which rhythms you are using and where you are in the bar, with the explicit rule that more notes per bar is not better than fewer \u2014 only different. 'More notes per bar is not better than a few, just different.' Chapter 1, p. 7.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "jerry-bergonzi:melodic-rhythms-vol-4:rhythmic-grammar"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "melodic-rhythms-vol-4",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "melodic-rhythms-vol-4",
          "citation": "chapter 1 p. 7"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "f",
      "granularity_name": "rhythmic-placement"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7",
      "function_role": "note-grouping-bar-line-independence",
      "narrative": "Bergonzi prescribes treating consecutive note groupings as independent of bar lines \u2014 playing a fixed number of notes 'within and over the bar, or through the bar.' The groupings need not even be connected; internal mental grouping affects articulation even when invisible to the listener. Chapter 1, p. 9-10.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "jerry-bergonzi:melodic-rhythms-vol-4:consecutive-note-displacement"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "melodic-rhythms-vol-4",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "melodic-rhythms-vol-4",
          "citation": "chapter 1 p. 9-10"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "f",
      "granularity_name": "rhythmic-placement"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7",
      "function_role": "five-note-grouping-duration-flexibility",
      "narrative": "Bergonzi prescribes that a five-note grouping may be played over 1 beat, 1\u00bd beats, 2 beats, 3 beats, or 4 beats \u2014 the same melodic cell flexibly rescaled to any duration over any harmonic context. 'The five notes can be played as 1 beat or 1 1/2 beats. 2 beats, 3 beats, or 4 beats, etc.' Chapter 1, p. 10.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "jerry-bergonzi:melodic-rhythms-vol-4:time-zone-gear-system"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "melodic-rhythms-vol-4",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "melodic-rhythms-vol-4",
          "citation": "chapter 1 p. 10"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "f",
      "granularity_name": "rhythmic-placement"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7",
      "function_role": "half-beat-displacement-rhythmic-variety",
      "narrative": "Bergonzi prescribes displacing one-bar rhythms by 1/2 beat, 1\u00bd beats, or 2\u00bd beats to produce perceptually distinct rhythmic patterns over any chord quality. 'Notice that by displacing a rhythm 1/2 of a beat, or 1 1/2 beats, or 2 1/2 beats, you create entirely different sounding rhythms at times.' Chapter 2, p. 16.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "jerry-bergonzi:melodic-rhythms-vol-4:consecutive-note-displacement"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "melodic-rhythms-vol-4",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "melodic-rhythms-vol-4",
          "citation": "chapter 2 p. 16"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "f",
      "granularity_name": "rhythmic-placement"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7",
      "function_role": "superimposition-cross-meter",
      "narrative": "Bergonzi prescribes superimposing odd-meter groupings (3/4, 5/4, 7/4, 9/4, 11/4) over a 4/4 foundation as a systematic method for generating rhythmic variety over any harmonic context. The 5/4-over-4/4 cycle aligns on bar 6; the 7/4 cycle aligns on bar 8. 'This is a 3/4 pattern which takes three bars of 4/4 to work out.' Chapter 3, p. 27; alignment rules at Chapter 15, pp. 69, 71.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "jerry-bergonzi:melodic-rhythms-vol-4:consecutive-note-displacement"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "melodic-rhythms-vol-4",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "melodic-rhythms-vol-4",
          "citation": "chapter 3 p. 27"
        },
        {
          "source": "melodic-rhythms-vol-4",
          "citation": "chapter 15 p. 69"
        },
        {
          "source": "melodic-rhythms-vol-4",
          "citation": "chapter 15 p. 71"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "f",
      "granularity_name": "rhythmic-placement"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7",
      "function_role": "hemiola-phrase-independence",
      "narrative": "Bergonzi prescribes playing 3/4 rhythmic phrases over 4/4 tunes (hemiola) to ensure phrases begin from various beats rather than always landing on beat one, developing bar-line independence and horizontal playing. 'Practicing this rhythmic technique helps the improviser to become independent of bar lines and makes one's playing more horizontal... It takes four bars of 3/4 to equal three bars of 4/4.' Chapter 13, p. 63.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "jerry-bergonzi:melodic-rhythms-vol-4:polyrhythmic-construction"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "melodic-rhythms-vol-4",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "melodic-rhythms-vol-4",
          "citation": "chapter 13 p. 63"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "f",
      "granularity_name": "rhythmic-placement"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7",
      "function_role": "hemiola-physical-practice-method",
      "narrative": "Bergonzi prescribes practicing hemiola (3/4 over 4/4) with a play-along while the right foot taps beat one and the left foot taps beat 3, combined with singing, to physically internalize the cross-rhythm. 'Try to practice these rhythms with a play along and also without a play along while your right foot taps beat one and your left foot taps beat 3. Also, try singing.' Chapter 13, p. 63.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "jerry-bergonzi:melodic-rhythms-vol-4:polyrhythmic-construction"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "melodic-rhythms-vol-4",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "melodic-rhythms-vol-4",
          "citation": "chapter 13 p. 63"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "f",
      "granularity_name": "rhythmic-placement"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7",
      "function_role": "sixteenth-note-conversion-from-eighth-notes",
      "narrative": "Bergonzi prescribes converting consecutive eighth-note exercises into sixteenth-note (double-time) exercises by doubling up; the resulting 2/4 phrase is then repeated twice to fill a 4/4 bar. 'Take the material presented in Chapters 3-8 on consecutive eighth notes and convert them to sixteenth-note exercises by doubling up... repeat the 2/4 phrase twice to make a 4/4 bar.' Chapter 13, p. 62.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "jerry-bergonzi:melodic-rhythms-vol-4:consecutive-note-displacement"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "melodic-rhythms-vol-4",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "melodic-rhythms-vol-4",
          "citation": "chapter 13 p. 62"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "i",
      "granularity_name": "other"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7",
      "function_role": "hemioval-mirror-rhythm-construction",
      "narrative": "Bergonzi prescribes generating a 5/4 mirror rhythm (hemioval) by restating the same rhythm on beat 2-and that was started on beat 1, and a 7/4 mirror rhythm by restating on beat 3-and. 'By starting the same rhythm on beat 2 \"and\" that was started on beat 1, you get a 5/4 mirror rhythm.' Chapter 16, p. 72.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "jerry-bergonzi:melodic-rhythms-vol-4:consecutive-note-displacement"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "melodic-rhythms-vol-4",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "melodic-rhythms-vol-4",
          "citation": "chapter 16 p. 72"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "f",
      "granularity_name": "rhythmic-placement"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7",
      "function_role": "triplet-practice-for-pocket-internalisation",
      "narrative": "Bergonzi prescribes practicing explicit triplet rhythms on a tune and then switching to eighth notes on the same tune, in order to internalize the pocket through felt contrast. 'Practicing playing triplets is one way to get more connected to the rhythm section groove and so it can help to improve your placement of eighth notes. Try playing these triplet rhythms on Tune 8 (One Heart) and then play eighth notes to see if you feel a different pocket.' Chapter 18, p. 77.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "jerry-bergonzi:melodic-rhythms-vol-4:time-zone-gear-system"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "melodic-rhythms-vol-4",
